# shir_nodes/take_lines.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Take first/last N lines or bytes (lowered from `head`/`tail`).
#
# Backends: Perl lines().take(), JS split().slice(), C fgets, Rust lines()
# Fallback: sh2.takeLines(text, count, from_end, bytes)

node TakeLines
tag "TakeLines"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field count: expr
field from_end: bool
field bytes: bool
# Spec: TextOps#take_lines_sound (docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Strings.thy,
# section "TakeLines: head/tail as an LF-budget cut"). The model is byte level:
# `count_lf` counts LF bytes, `nlines s = 1 + count_lf s`, and `cut n s` is "cut the
# text at the Nth LF and drop that LF". The theorem states, for every text and
# count: `cut 0 s = []`; empty input is ONE line (`nlines [] = 1`) and a bare
# terminator is two (`nlines [LF] = 2`); the output is a prefix of the input and no
# longer than it; a text with fewer LFs than the count comes back unchanged
# (`count_lf s < n ⟹ cut n s = s`) and otherwise the input IS the output followed by
# a dropped terminator and a remainder (`s = cut n s @ LF # r`) — the truncation law
# `head` needs, with the last kept line's terminator NOT retained; the number of
# terminators left is `min (n - 1) (count_lf s)`, so `0 < n ⟹ nlines (cut n s) =
# min n (nlines s)`. The from-end arm is `drop_lf`, proved to remove exactly the
# terminators it is asked for (`count_lf (drop_lf k s) = count_lf s - k`) and to
# leave a text shorter than the count unchanged. Byte mode is modelled as a plain
# slice and only its length is claimed.
#
# NOT PROMISED (stated, not assumed): negative and non-numeric counts (the lowering
# parses an i64; GNU's `head -n -5` means "all but the last five" and these renderers
# do not, so the model takes a nat — huge counts simply hit the unchanged case); byte
# mode, which the lowering refuses today; any encoding beyond byte pass-through (bytes
# are compared with 10 only, so UTF-8 and invalid UTF-8 pass through and CR, CRLF and
# NUL are ordinary bytes — a CRLF line keeps its CR, as the witnesses show); the
# host/process behaviour of head/tail (files, -q, -v, SIGPIPE, exit status); and the
# byte-level statement that the from-end output is a SUFFIX of the input, which needs
# an associativity witness and is left as an open refinement.
spec: TextOps#take_lines_sound
